{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And you shall love HaShem your Elohim.</b> Since we have no Elohim but Him alone, you are obligated to love Him and to fulfill the commandments out of love, for one who fulfills them out of fear — his service is not complete service. The meaning of love is that a person reflects upon His Torah and His commandments and attains through them knowledge of the blessed Name, and delights in that attainment with the utmost delight. And therefore it immediately follows, ״והיו הדברים האלה״ — it thereby explains the nature of love for the blessed Name, what it is. Thus they expounded in the Sifrei: ״ואהבת את ה׳ אלהיך״ — I do not know how a person loves HaShem; Scripture comes to teach, ״והיו הדברים האלה״. ״ואהבת את ה׳ אלהיך״ — love Him before the creatures, in the manner that Abraham did, whom the Holy One, blessed be He, called (Isaiah [Kings I 8:31]) ״אברהם אוהבי״ — 'Abraham My beloved.' The Sages, blessed be their memory, explained to us in this matter that the meaning of love for the Name, blessed be He, is one who justifies the many and draws them near to the life of the World to Come, and therefore they brought a proof from Abraham. And if you ask and say: since we find that the rank of חשק (passionate attachment) is greater than the rank of love, it should have said וחשקת — why did it say ואהבת? The answer is that this very point is the reason, for one who loves is one who loves the beloved thing, yet at times the love departs from him, for example at the time of eating or at the time of sleep; but one who is bound by חשק — his thought is never separated from the object of his attachment at all, and even at the time he eats he remembers it, and even at the time of sleep he sees it in a dream. And since we are commanded to love HaShem, blessed be He, with both inclinations, and with the desiring soul, and with all his wealth — and a person needs to love all these things that are necessities, for it is impossible for him to live without them — therefore He commanded that he love them in the manner of necessity, but that he love HaShem, blessed be He, above them all. If so, for this reason it could not have said וחשקת, for if he were bound by חשק he would separate his thought from all these physical things and not use them at all, even the necessary ones, and there would be no place for the commandments of the Torah at all. For the term חשק means cleaving (דבקות), in the manner of what is said (Psalms 91:14): ״כי בי חשק ואפלטהו״, and this is the rank of death by the kiss (מיתת הנשיקה), among the wholly righteous. And so we find regarding the דבקות of HaShem, blessed be He, with Israel, that it is written (Deuteronomy 7:7): ״לא מרבכם מכל העמים חשק ה׳ בכם״ — and Scripture teaches us an intensification regarding the דבקות of the Shekhinah with Israel, that it never departs from them, in the manner of what the Sages, blessed be their memory, expounded: 'Every place where Israel was exiled, the Shekhinah was with them — they were exiled to Egypt, the Shekhinah was with them,' and so in all the exiles." "<b>With all your heart.</b> The heart is the dwelling-place of the soul's wisdom. And since it is the first organ formed in the creation of the human being and the last to die among all the organs of the body, therefore it says 'with all your heart' — that is, until the last moment of death." "<b>And with all your soul.</b> This is the desiring soul. <b>And with all your might.</b> From the word מאד — that is, love Him greatly with all that you can. The meaning is that his wealth should not be more precious to him than the commandments, for it is not fitting for him to be sparing with the outlay of money for a commandment: even if he found a lulav and etrog only at a high price, he should not be sparing with his money but should take it, as Rabban Gamliel did, who bought one for a thousand zuz. And our Rabbis, blessed be their memory, already said: הידור מצוה — beautifying a commandment — up to a third in the commandment. This is what they said in Bava Kamma: Rabbi Zeira said in the name of Rav Huna: הדור מצוה עד שליש. What is 'a third'? If you say a third of his house — if three commandments chanced to come his way, he would give his entire house for them! Rather say: הדור מצוה עד שליש במצוה — that is, he may add up to a third of its price, whether between a beautified commandment and a more beautified one, whether a Torah scroll or a fine lulav toward a finer one, as is taught (Exodus 15:2): ״זה אלי ואנוהו״ — 'This is my El and I will beautify for Him' — adorn yourself before Him in commandments. How so? That he should have before him a beautiful Torah scroll, a beautiful lulav, beautiful tzitzit. And the halakhah follows Rabbi Zeira. And our Rabbis, blessed be their memory, expounded: בכל לבבך — with both your inclinations, the good inclination and the evil inclination; בכל נפשך — even if He takes your soul; ובכל מאדך — with all your wealth. If there is a person whose body is more precious to him than his wealth, therefore it says בכל נפשך; and there is a person whose wealth is more precious to him than his body, therefore it says בכל מאדך. Another interpretation: בכל מאדך — in every measure (מדה) that He measures out to you. This is what David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 56:11): ״באלהים אהלל דבר בה׳ אהלל דבר״ — whether with the measure of judgment or with the measure of mercy, I will praise the word. And likewise (Psalms 101:1): ״חסד ומשפט אשירה״ — whether kindness or justice, I will sing. And likewise (Psalms 116:13): ״כוס ישועות אשא ובשם ה׳ אקרא״ — I will lift the cup of salvation and call upon the Name of HaShem; (ibid. 116:3–4) ״צרה ויגון אמצא ובשם ה׳ אקרא״ — distress and sorrow I find, and I call upon the Name of HaShem. And there are those who further interpreted: בכל לבבך ובכל נפשך ובכל מאדך — corresponding to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob: בכל לבבך corresponding to Abraham, who loved the Holy One, blessed be He, with all his heart and drew hearts to faith in Him; ובכל נפשך corresponding to Isaac, who surrendered his soul upon the altar; ובכל מאדך corresponding to Jacob, who separated a tithe from all his wealth and even from his sons."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "Citation '(ישעיהו מלכים א ח׳:ל״י)' appears to conflate Isaiah with II Chronicles or I Kings 8:31; the verse referenced is likely II Chronicles 20:7 or Isaiah 41:8 calling Abraham 'My beloved'; translated as printed.", 2 "Psalm citations normalized: תהילים נ״ו:י״א = Psalms 56:11; שם קא = Psalms 101:1; שם קטז = Psalms 116. The closing verses from Psalms 116:3-4 are paraphrased midrashically rather than quoted verbatim; translated as R. Bahya renders them."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:46:52.205058034Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}